@page "/about"
@model LutheranMusicLibrary.Web.Pages.About.IndexModel
@{ ViewData["Title"] = "About"; }

<div class="page-header">
    <h1>About Hymn Desk</h1>
</div>

<div class="homepage-intro" style="text-align:left; max-width:720px;">
    <p>
        Hymn Desk grew out of years spent working as a church musician — moving between hymnals,
        chasing down scripture connections, and building the kind of cross-referenced index I kept
        wishing already existed. What started as a simple browser-based planner for my own use slowly
        turned into a full reference database: thousands of hymn texts and tunes, cross-referenced
        across nineteen hymnals, tied to scripture, the church year, and the Lutheran confessions.
    </p>
    <p>
        It's built like a wiki on purpose. Everyone already knows how to use one — click a link,
        land on a page, click another link, keep going — and that felt like the right shape for a
        resource like this: something a working church musician can fall into for five minutes or
        an hour, chasing one hymn to its tune, its translator, its scripture roots, and back out again.
    </p>
    <p>
        There are other hymn reference tools out there. This one exists because, using those others,
        I kept thinking: <em>I can make this better</em>, or <em>why is this missing?</em> Hymn Desk is
        my attempt to build the tool I wanted to have.
    </p>
    <p>
        This is a personal project, not a commercial product, and not affiliated with any publisher,
        denomination, or hymnal committee. It's a work in progress — new pages, corrections, and
        features are added regularly.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Acknowledgments</h2>
    <p>
        Hymn texts, tunes, and cross-references draw on public-domain sources and a wide range of
        published hymnals; individual hymn pages note hymnal cross-references where known. Theological
        and historical notes on the Psalter Hymnal Handbook (PHH) are paraphrased, not reproduced.
        Scripture references use public-domain translations. See the <a href="/resources">Resources</a>
        page for the external sites this project regularly draws on and links out to.
    </p>
</div>
